#: src/3678-final.md:1
msgid "Feature Name: `final`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:2
msgid "Start Date: 2024-07-20"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:3
msgid ""
"RFC PR: [rust-lang/rfcs#3678](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3678)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:4
msgid ""
"Rust Issue: [rust-lang/rust#131179](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/131179)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:6
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:9
msgid ""
"Support restricting implementation of individual methods within traits, "
"using the existing unused `final` keyword."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:12
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:15
msgid ""
"When defining a trait, the trait can provide optional methods with default "
"implementations, which become available on every implementation of the "
"trait. However, the implementer of the trait can still provide their own "
"implementation of such a method. In some cases, the trait does not want to "
"allow implementations to vary, and instead wants to guarantee that all "
"implementations of the trait use an identical method implementation. For "
"instance, this may be an assumption required for correctness."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:23
msgid "This RFC allows restricting the implementation of trait methods."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:25
msgid ""
"This mechanism also faciliates marker-like traits providing no implementable "
"methods, such that implementers only choose whether to provide the trait and "
"never how to implement it; the trait then provides all the method "
"implementations."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:30
msgid ""
"One example of a trait in the standard library benefiting from this: `Error::"
"type_id`, which has thus far remained unstable because it's unsafe to "
"override. This RFC would allow stabilizing that method so users can call it, "
"without permitting reimplementation of it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:35
msgid ""
"Another would be the `Read::read_buf_exact` method. Making this `final` "
"would allow callers to rely on its implementation to be correct, while "
"keeping the function safe to call. Without this, callers using `unsafe` code "
"must defend against the possibility of an incorrect `read_buf_exact` "
"implementation (e.g. returning `Ok(())` without filling the buffer) to avoid "
"UB."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:41
msgid "Explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:44
msgid ""
"When defining a trait, the definition can annotate methods or associated "
"functions to restrict whether implementations of the trait can define them. "
"For instance:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:51
msgid "\"MyTrait::method: {self}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:56
msgid ""
"A method or associated function marked as `final` must have a default body."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:58
msgid ""
"When implementing a trait, the compiler will emit an error if the "
"implementation attempts to define any method or associated function marked "
"as `final`, and will emit a suggestion to delete the implementation."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:62
msgid ""
"In every other way, an `final` method or associated function acts "
"identically to any other method or associated function, and can be invoked "
"accordingly:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:71
msgid ""
"Note that in some cases, the compiler might choose to avoid placing a "
"`final` method in the trait's vtable, if the one-and-only implementation "
"does not benefit from monomorphization."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:75
msgid ""
"Note that removing a `final` restriction is a compatible change. (Removing a "
"default implementation remains a breaking change.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:78
msgid "Reference-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:81
msgid "At runtime, a `final fn` behaves exactly the same as a `fn`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:83
msgid ""
"Removing `final` may be a non-breaking change. (If `final` was preventing "
"implementation to prevent a soundness issue, though, this would require "
"additional care.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:87
msgid ""
"Adding `final` is a breaking change, unless the trait already did not allow "
"third-party implementations (such as via a sealed trait)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:90
msgid ""
"At compile-time, a method declared as `final fn` in a trait must have a "
"provided body, and cannot be overridden in any `impl`, even an `impl` in the "
"same crate or module."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:94
msgid "`final fn` cannot be combined with `default fn`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:96
msgid ""
"`final` is only allowed in trait definitions. `final` is not allowed on "
"impls or their items, non-trait functions, or `extern` blocks."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:99
msgid ""
"A `final fn` never prevents a trait from having `dyn`\\-compatibility; the "
"trait can remain `dyn`\\-compatible as long as all non-`final` methods "
"support `dyn`\\-compatibility. This also means that a `final fn` can always "
"be called on a `dyn Trait`, even if the same method as a non-`final` `fn` "
"would not have been `dyn`\\-compatible."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:105
msgid "Drawbacks"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:108
msgid ""
"As with any language feature, this adds more surface area to the language."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:110
msgid "Rationale and alternatives"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:113
msgid ""
"Instead of or in addition to this, we could allow inherent `impl` blocks for "
"a `Trait` (e.g. `impl Trait { ... }` without `for Type`). People today "
"already occasionally write `impl dyn Trait` blocks, since `dyn Trait` is a "
"type and supports inherent impl blocks; this change would allow generalizing "
"such blocks by deleting the `dyn`. This has the potential for conceptual "
"complexity or confusion for new users, as well as potentially affecting the "
"quality of diagnostics. (It also used to have a meaning in Rust 2015: the "
"same meaning `impl dyn Trait` now has.) However, it would provide "
"orthogonality, and an interesting conceptual model."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:123
msgid ""
"Rather than using `final`, we could use the `impl(visibility)` syntax from "
"[RFC 3323](https://rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/3323-restrictions.html). This "
"would allow more flexibility (such as overriding a method within the crate "
"but not outside the crate), and would be consistent with other uses of RFC "
"3323. On the other hand, such flexibility would come at the cost of "
"additional complexity. We can always add such syntax for the more general "
"cases in the future if needed; see the future possibilities section."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:131
msgid ""
"Rather than using `final`, we could use `#[final]`. This concept is somewhat "
"similar to \"final\" methods in other languages, and we already have the "
"`final` keyword reserved so we could use either an attribute or a keyword."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:136
msgid ""
"It's possible to work around the lack of this functionality by placing the "
"additional methods in an extension trait with a blanket implementation. "
"However, this is a user-visible API difference: the user must import the "
"extension trait, and use methods from the extension trait rather than from "
"the base trait."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:142
msgid "Prior art"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:145
msgid "This feature is similar to `final` methods in Java or C++."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:147
msgid ""
"It's also similar to `sealed` in C#, where `sealed class` is something from "
"which you can't derive and a base class can use `sealed` on a method to say "
"derived classes can't `override` it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:151
msgid "Unresolved questions"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:154
msgid "None yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:156
msgid "Future possibilities"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:159
msgid ""
"`final` methods do not need to appear in a trait's vtable. However, _if_ a "
"method is `dyn`\\-compatible, and if it would benefit from monomorphization, "
"we could optionally put it in the trait's vtable, perhaps with an explicit "
"option to do so."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:164
msgid ""
"We could allow `final fn` methods on `#[marker]` traits, which are currently "
"not allowed to have any methods (because they can't allow different "
"implementations in different `impl`s)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:168
msgid ""
"As mentioned in the alternatives section, we could allow inherent `impl` "
"blocks for a `Trait` (e.g. `impl Trait { ... }` without `for Type`). People "
"today already occasionally write `impl dyn Trait` blocks, since `dyn Trait` "
"is a type and supports inherent impl blocks; this change would allow "
"generalizing such blocks by deleting the `dyn`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:174
msgid ""
"When evaluating possible future syntaxes such as `impl Trait { ... }` "
"blocks, we should take into account:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:176
msgid "The conceptual model we want to present to users"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:177
msgid ""
"Whether we anticipate user confusion due to the former meaning of this "
"syntax in Rust 2015 (prior to the move from `Trait` to `dyn Trait` to write "
"trait objects)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:180
msgid "Any effect on diagnostic quality"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:181
msgid "Whether an additional syntax adds excessive implementation complexity"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:182
msgid ""
"How much we want the benefit of allowing `impl dyn Trait` blocks to be "
"generalized by deleting the `dyn`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:185
msgid ""
"We could add additional flexibility using the restriction mechanism defined "
"in [RFC 3323](https://rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/3323-restrictions.html), "
"using syntax like `impl(crate)` to restrict implementation of a method or "
"associated function outside a crate while allowing implementations within "
"the crate. (Likewise with `impl(self)` or any other visibility.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:191
msgid ""
"We could theoretically allow `final` restrictions on associated consts and "
"types, as well. If this is simple to implement, we should implement it for "
"all items that can appear in a trait simultaneously; if it proves difficult "
"to implement, we should prioritize methods."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:196
msgid ""
"We could support some syntax (e.g. `impl(unsafe)`), to make a method safe to "
"call, but unsafe to override. This would allow the implementation to be "
"trusted, so that unsafe code can rely on it rather than defending against "
"incorrect implementations."
msgstr ""

#: src/3678-final.md:201
msgid ""
"We could integrate this with stability markers, to stabilize calling a "
"method but keep it unstable to _implement_."
msgstr ""
